  "versions": [
    {
      "meaning": "Universality and the Created",
      "description": "The number four represents the created world and its universal extent. There are four corners of the earth, four winds, and four seasons. In the Garden of Eden, the river divided into four branches. Ezekiel's vision includes four living creatures that represent all of creation.",
      "reason": "[minor] The description is good but could benefit from mentioning several central biblical occurrences, such as the four night watches, God's four-letter name (JHVH), or the four coverings of the tabernacle. Some of these could fit as footnotes.; [minor] Footnotes are completely missing. The number 4 has rich symbolism in rabbinic and kabbalistic tradition (e.g., four worlds in Kabbalah, four sons in the Passover Haggadah, four letters in God's name) which would enrich the entry without disrupting the flow of the main text.; [minor] The 'Meaning' field \"Universality and the created\" is precise and comprehensive but could also include \"order of creation\" or \"completeness in the material\" for further precision.",
      "score": 6,
      "date": "2026-04-04"
    }
  ],
  "footnotes": [
    {
      "text": "In Kabbalistic tradition, the number four corresponds to the four worlds (olamot): Atzilut (emanation), Beriah (creation), Yetzirah (formation), and Assiyah (action). These represent different levels of reality from the divine to the material.",
      "source": "kabbalistisk"
    },
    {
      "text": "In the Passover Haggadah, four sons appear who represent different approaches to faith: the wise one, the wicked one, the simple one, and the one who does not know how to ask. This pattern highlights the function of the number four as an expression of wholeness and diversity.",
      "source": "rabbinsk"
    },
    {
      "text": "God's name YHWH (yod-he-vav-he) consists of four letters and is therefore called the Tetragrammaton (from Greek 'four letters'). In Jewish interpretive tradition, these four letters have been linked to the four elements and the four corners of the earth.",
      "source": "lingvistisk"
    },
    {
      "text": "The Romans divided the night into four watches, a practice reflected in the New Testament (Mark 13:35). In Jewish temple tradition, the night was divided into three watches, but during Jesus' time, the Roman division into four was common in Israel.",
      "source": "historisk"
    }
  ]
